mark_scheme.text:
Survey plan (reference):
1. Survey purpose: To understand the after-school reading situation of students in this school, and to compare the differences in reading hobbies and reading amounts between boys and girls.
2. Survey objects and scope: current students of this school (can be limited to a certain grade or the whole school).
3. Sampling method: stratified random sampling. Stratify by grade (or class) and randomly select a number of students from each stratum; at the same time, ensure that the number of boys and girls is roughly equal (or selected according to the actual proportion). The sample size is recommended to be ≥100 (or no less than 30 students per gender per grade).
4. Survey tool: Anonymous questionnaire (paper/online) + interview verification when necessary.
5. Main survey indicators (examples of core questions in the questionnaire):
  (1) Gender, grade/class;
  (2) Daily/weekly after-school reading time (fill in numbers or bins: 0–15, 16–30, 31–60, >60 minutes);
  (3) Number of books read per month (paper/electronic statistics separately or total);
  (4) Reading content preferences (multiple choices: literature/classics, popular science, history, comics, online novels, study guidance, etc.);
  (5) Reading methods and sources (paper/electronic; library/purchase/borrowing/online);
  (6) Motivations and barriers to reading (multiple choices available).
6. Implementation steps:
  (1) Design and pre-survey (small sample test, modify questions);
  (2) Formal distribution and recycling (unified time to ensure anonymity and voluntariness);
  (3) Data sorting (eliminating invalid questionnaires and coding entry).
7. Data analysis and comparison:
  (1) Descriptive statistics: Calculate the average reading time/number of books read and the ratio of grades for boys and girls respectively;
  (2) Differences in reading hobbies: Make a contingency table of male and female genders for "preference categories" and compare the proportions of various choices (chi-square test or direct comparison of percentages can be used);
  (3) Difference in reading amount: compare the average reading time/number of books between boys and girls (t test or comparison of medians and distributions can be used);
  (4) Result presentation: Use bar chart/pie chart/box plot to display, and give a conclusion "whether there is a difference and in what aspects the difference is mainly reflected."
8. Things to note: Protect privacy, state the question clearly, avoid misleading options, and the sample should be representative.

